{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe CI repair loop cannot fix lint failures in any project that is not Python. When `run_checks` fails, `apply_ci_repair` short-circuits into a hardcoded autofix that shells out to `ruff check --fix .` on the activity worker. Three defects follow from that. It is hardcoded to one language, it measures success as \"files changed\" rather than \"the failure is fixed\", and it is unscoped, so it rewrites the whole tree.\n\nRunning the platform against the `archive-massgov-pfml` portal made the damage visible. Prettier flagged one TypeScript file, and the repair loop instead modified 16 unrelated Python files across five attempts with `llm_calls=0` on every attempt, so no agent ever saw the failure.\n\n## Approach\n\nLet each project declare its own fix command in its checks config, and run that command in the same pod and image the checks already run in, so the fixer sees the toolchain the checks saw.\n\nThis fits because the platform already solved the identical problem for the check itself. `services/orchestration/checks/runner.py` builds a script and executes it through `gates/pod_runner.py`, so the fix path reuses that transport rather than inventing a second one. A project-declared command is also the only option that generalises across client tech stacks. The platform cannot know whether a repo wants `ruff`, `prettier --write`, `gofmt`, or a bespoke `make fix`, but the repo already knows.\n\nWhen a project declares no `fix_command`, the behaviour falls back to agent-driven repair rather than doing nothing, so no existing project regresses.\n\n### Bounding what a fix may write back\n\nA verified fix is copied back only for files the failure summary named. `failing_files` extracts those paths and `in_repair_scope` tests each changed file against them.\n\nAn empty scope is fail-closed. When the failure summary names no parseable path, no file is copied back and the repair degrades to agent repair. Treating an empty scope as \"everything matches\" would reintroduce the whole-tree rewrite this ticket sets out to remove, because a project fix command such as `npm run lint:fix` reformats every file it is pointed at, and a summary such as `[warn] Code style issues found in the above file(s).` names none of them. Fail-closed costs one autofix opportunity in that case and still reaches a fix through the agent, whereas fail-open costs an unbounded diff.\n\n### Observability\n\nEvery outcome of the fix path is recorded, because the motivating incident was invisible. `_project_autofix` emits a structured log naming which path it took, one of `applied`, `no-fix-command`, `fix-exited-non-zero`, `pod-failed`, `empty-scope`, or `path-rejected`. Without this, the degrade-to-agent-repair transitions are swallowed exactly as the original defect was.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `services/orchestration/checks/config.py` parses the new `fix_command` key.\n- `services/orchestration/checks/runner.py` gains `run_fix`, `parse_fix_output`, and the fix script builder, alongside the existing check runner.\n- `services/orchestration/temporal_activities/repair.py` deletes `_autofix_lint` and adds `_project_autofix`, `_apply_staged_files`, and `_confined`, and emits the outcome log.\n- `services/orchestration/langgraph/ci_repair.py` gains `failing_files` and `in_repair_scope`, and its LINT instruction takes a `{fix_hint}`.\n- `services/orchestration/gates/pod_runner.py` gains a `privileged` flag and `copy_out`, because buildah cannot unpack a layer unprivileged under k3s.\n- `scripts/config.py` gains `gate_engine_privileged`.\n- `services/orchestration/gates/builtin.py`, `services/orchestration/temporal_activities/checks.py`, and `services/orchestration/temporal_workflows/draft_pull_request.py` thread the new arguments.\n- `config/local/checks.yaml.example` documents the key.\n- Tests: `tests/unit/test_ci_repair_project_fix.py` (new), `tests/unit/test_ci_repair_planner.py`, `tests/unit/test_gate_pod_runner.py`. `tests/unit/test_ci_repair_autofix.py` is deleted with the code it covered.\n\n## Grounded assumptions\n\n- The repair path, not `run_checks`, is where a fix must land, because `commit_and_push` only fires after `repair.applied` in the `_repair` branch of `services/orchestration/temporal_workflows/draft_pull_request.py`.\n- Checks already execute inside a pod via `PodOps` in `services/orchestration/gates/pod_runner.py`, so the fix can reuse that transport.\n- Buildah requires `privileged: true` under k3s on Colima. Four probe pods confirmed that `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` alone and `CAP_SYS_ADMIN` with seccomp Unconfined both fail, because unpacking a layer remounts the container root `MS_PRIVATE|MS_REC`.\n\n## Testing\n\nHappy path: a project declares `fix_command`, the lint check fails, the fix runs in the pod, and the changed files are copied back and staged. Asserted by `tests/unit/test_ci_repair_project_fix.py`.\n\nFailure and edge paths: a project that declares no `fix_command` falls through to agent repair. A fix command that exits non-zero, or a pod that fails outright, degrades to agent repair rather than killing the workflow. A failure summary naming no path copies back no file and degrades to agent repair. A fix that touches a path outside the repair scope has that path rejected by `_confined`. A `tar` that reports a file removed by the fix command does not fail the copy-out.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `ruff check --fix .` no longer appears anywhere in `services/`, verified by `! grep -rq \"ruff check --fix\" services/` exiting 0.\n- [ ] A project that declares `fix_command` has that command run instead, verified by `tests/unit/test_ci_repair_project_fix.py` passing.\n- [ ] A project that declares no `fix_command` reaches agent repair, verified by a unit test asserting the agent repair path is taken.\n- [ ] A pod failure during the fix does not fail the workflow, verified by a unit test that raises from the pod runner and asserts the agent repair path is taken.\n- [ ] A failure summary naming no parseable path copies back zero files, verified by a unit test asserting `_apply_staged_files` returns 0 for an empty scope.\n- [ ] `_project_autofix` records which outcome it took, verified by a unit test asserting the structured log names `no-fix-command` when a project declares no fix command.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A live run against `archive-massgov-pfml` reports `applied: true`.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] That same live run modifies exactly the one file prettier flagged.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] That same live run reports `llm_calls: 0`.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] A re-run of the checks after that live run exits 0.\n- [ ] `ruff check services tests scripts adapters` exits 0."}},"env_id":"8c3ef925-fbdc-4d88-8d59-f391cf59568c","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789441637181288001,"uuid":"684c166a-2040-4097-8b34-12f79699c00d"}